what the bench solves.

```
V_pcc  =  Vs + R·P + X·Q                      network coupling
P_ref  =  P0 − (kp/100)·dz(f − 60, db_f)      frequency-watt
Q_ref  =  −(kq/100)·dz(V − Vref, db_v)/0.01   volt-var
Tp·dP  =  P_ref − P     Tq·dQ = Q_ref − Q     open-loop response time
2H·dω  =  Pg + r·ΔP − ΔP_load − D·ω           swing equation
Tg·dPg =  −Pg − Kg·ω                          rest-of-fleet governor
dz(x,db) = x−db (x>db), x+db (x<−db), else 0  deadband
```

per unit on the inverter rating. the grid is a thevenin source behind R + jX. the voltage at the
PCC is the source plus the rise the plant makes itself — so volt-var closes a loop through the
network it is measuring. loop gain is `X · dQ/dV`. raise kq on a weak line and the answer starts
to argue with the question. hunting.

three things to watch, in order.

- **nadir** — how far frequency falls before the fleet catches it. droop gain and response time.
- **excursion** — how far voltage strays at the PCC. kq, deadband, and the reactance you are behind.
- **ripple** — whether the response settles or oscillates. loop gain against response time.

ieee 1547-2018 defaults live in the bench as a preset: deadband 0.036 Hz, 5 % droop (33.3 % of
rating per hertz), open-loop response 0.2–10 s, category B reactive capability 0.44 pu. rule 21
uses a piecewise volt-var curve; the single slope here is its linearised cousin.

